WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
Film Courage: In the beginning of&nbsp;
your writing career how are you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.940
green on the business side of being a TV writer?

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:11.400
Niceole R. Levy, Screenwriter/Author: Oh my God, I&nbsp;
was so green about the whole thing. When you first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:17.880
break in The Writer's Guild one of the main&nbsp;
things that they do for us is negotiate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:26.160
MBA which is our minimum basic agreement with the&nbsp;
studios, so there's set amounts that staff writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.540
will make. It's written down it's like they're in&nbsp;
a piece of paper so you don't have to there's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:37.500
a ton of negotiation. When you're a staff writer&nbsp;
but as you go on like all of this stuff gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:44.340
very complicated and there's issues of how long a&nbsp;
studio can hold you and how much more they have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:50.220
pay you to hold you and it gets very complicated,&nbsp;
which is why having a rep that you can communicate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:55.320
with is so important because I have literally just&nbsp;
had to say to my agent my manager sometimes like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:06.000
what does this mean I don't understand this&nbsp;
what does this mean. Just in terms of how how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.480
your career is going to build it's sort of like&nbsp;
an idea in the beginning but it really is sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:20.820
one job at a time one project at a time because&nbsp;
my reps would tell you every time a project ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:25.440
I'm like “You're going to be able to find me&nbsp;
another job right?” and they're like “Girl of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.420
course you're going to find another job,” but I&nbsp;
can't believe my own press so I'm always like am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:37.680
I going to want another job though but it's paying&nbsp;
attention to the business paying attention to all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:45.240
this consolidation that's going on are the people&nbsp;
that you knew who worked at Netflix getting laid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.340
off or did they survive the layoffs because&nbsp;
sometimes you're reaching out to the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.560
to say like hey I have this project and I'm&nbsp;
looking for someone who might be interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:01.200
in it does it seem to you like one of your overall&nbsp;
people might be interested in this or something to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:07.680
your friends and if your friends are in these mass&nbsp;
layoffs now they're off looking for different work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:15.300
how systems change having worked at Marvel&nbsp;
TV in its old incarnation and then that whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:21.000
thing going away so everybody I knew who worked&nbsp;
for Marvel didn't work for Marvel anymore in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:27.660
matter of a year so the whole marble TV landscape&nbsp;
has changed and so it's like okay who who does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:32.520
work over there do I know anyone there so you're&nbsp;
you're constantly having to pay attention to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:37.200
evolution of this business there are people who&nbsp;
choose not to there are people who are like that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.760
just too much for me I don't want to deal with&nbsp;
it I like to be informed about all those things I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:49.320
think it's really important to me so that when my&nbsp;
agent sends me something from a particular studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:55.920
or a producing pod that I am like oh they're&nbsp;
connected to this place I don't really love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:02.820
maybe I don't want to look at this or oh I love&nbsp;
that place so yes let's definitely work there so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:07.980
it I just think that edge you're always educating&nbsp;
yourself not just about your skill set that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:14.940
need as a writer but about how to be how to be a&nbsp;
show runner in the future about how this business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:22.920
works about what good representation looks like I&nbsp;
constantly have younger writers saying to me like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.600
having this problem with my agent but I don't&nbsp;
want to say anything and I'm like no no your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:33.540
agent works for you they work for you you want&nbsp;
to have a good partnership with them but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:38.340
get paid because you get paid so it's fine to go&nbsp;
to them and say hey I'm a little concerned about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:44.820
X can we talk about it and it's it's fascinating&nbsp;
how many younger writers are scared to have those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:53.400
conversations so we're we're all encouraging them&nbsp;
to go do that there's a there's a lot about it I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:59.100
still don't know if you ask me to explain to&nbsp;
you half the stuff in my feature contract I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:04.020
would tell you I would need to talk to my future&nbsp;
agent again because she explained it all to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:09.000
and I remember half of it but a whole bunch of&nbsp;
it I'm like oh I don't actually remember how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.980
that bonus gets triggered I would have to ask&nbsp;
my feature agent so it's a lot and it's really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.400
important to work with people that you feel like&nbsp;
you can trust who are giving you the best advice.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:23.040
Film Courage: And a younger writer&nbsp;
would be scared to ask or push the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.880
envelope because they feel like I'm finally&nbsp;
in the door I don't want to jeopardize it?

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:32.160
Niceole: That's it absolutely. Sometimes it&nbsp;
takes so long to get wrapped it's so hard to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.020
representation and they get scared of rocking the&nbsp;
boat because they don't want to be without a rep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:43.920
I had a conversation with a writer who gave me&nbsp;
really wonderful advice and it was that it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:50.880
honestly better to be unrepresented than to have&nbsp;
the wrong rep because the wrong rep is holding you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.400
back like it sucks to not be represented because&nbsp;
there's stuff it's harder to submit for you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:03.180
to try to like can your lawyer submit you if you&nbsp;
have a lawyer it all gets very tricky but if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:08.760
have a rep who isn't doing the work isn't sending&nbsp;
you out on general meetings so that you can build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:13.920
a reputation amongst executives and amongst&nbsp;
producers of someone they want to work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:22.200
they're not helping you build your career and&nbsp;
so it's fine to say look I totally get that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:28.860
you're super busy could you maybe put someone&nbsp;
more junior on my team who can help me set up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:34.800
more general meetings because I really want&nbsp;
to get to co-EP [executive produce] in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:40.620
next five years and so I need to get out there&nbsp;
and meet these people and be on that path it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:45.120
perfectly fine to say that because you can frame&nbsp;
it in a way that's not you're not doing your job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.940
it's I totally get that you have bigger clients&nbsp;
than me so would it be okay if we tried this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.020
and if they say no they're not the&nbsp;
right rep for you they're just not.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:06:00.180
Film Courage: Tips for the TV writer's&nbsp;
room, like things that a new writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:05.760
might not think of in terms of maybe&nbsp;
where to sit what if they're fidgety?

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:12.540
Niceole: Yes there there are so many things that&nbsp;
I feel like I talk about in the Writer’s Survival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:13.259
Guide [Niceole’s book, THE WRITER'S ROOM SURVIVAL&nbsp;
GUIDE: Don’t Screw up the lunch order and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.259 --> 00:06:15.180
keys to a happy Writers' Room from Michael Wiese&nbsp;
Production] that will seem silly to people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:22.080
have never worked in a writer's room but they're&nbsp;
so important and so for instance the the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:27.600
day you get there and it's sort of like where does&nbsp;
everybody sit if it's a returning show there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:32.100
probably people who already have seats that they&nbsp;
like so kind of like hang back and see what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:37.320
going on or you can say does anybody sit here&nbsp;
and they'll tell you if someone does or not right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:44.100
if it's if you're the kind of writer who&nbsp;
is very fidgety and like you like to draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:51.240
constantly or you're like a leg bouncer and&nbsp;
you need to be bouncing your leg or you take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:57.060
pens apart I've worked with writers who do that&nbsp;
don't sit by your show runner because your show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:01.500
runner's attention is going to constantly be&nbsp;
pulled to you and that might be a problem for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:06.720
you so sit across the room just sit across the&nbsp;
room and do your thing you got to do to feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:12.120
comfortable but it won't be right in your show&nbsp;
runner's face if you are the kind of writer who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:18.540
has to go to the bathroom every hour sit closer&nbsp;
to the door so you can just get up and slip out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:23.340
and come back in and it won't be any big drama&nbsp;
because no one wants to be the person who has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:28.620
to walk across the entire writer's room every&nbsp;
hour it's like it's you don't nobody wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:36.180
do it so just save yourself the problem when&nbsp;
it comes to lunch orders this is a big one so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:41.400
in a lot of rooms the studio will provide lunch&nbsp;
for the writers when you're meeting in person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:49.200
and there's limits to how much you can spend and&nbsp;
so there's usually like a an assembly of menus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:55.500
of places that you can go to right if it's your&nbsp;
day to pick and you don't want to pick just pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.500 --> 00:08:00.360
it on just be like I don't want to pick today&nbsp;
someone else pick if it's your day to pick and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:06.060
that the place you want to go is a place that&nbsp;
everybody hates please do not pick that place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:13.620
because they're all going to be mad at you if you&nbsp;
notice that your show runner's a little grumpy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:20.160
today and what his favorite place is pick his&nbsp;
favorite place because it's like he's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:24.240
be real happy when he's like oh we're going there&nbsp;
today great and suddenly his mood gets better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:31.740
so it's just it's a lot of silly kind of&nbsp;
little things but because it's a room full of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:37.020
personalities that have to find a way to coexist&nbsp;
together those things can be really important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:43.200
so I I hopefully people will think about those&nbsp;
things when they walk in there for the first time.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:48.080
Film Courage: Are writing credits your&nbsp;
ticket to getting better and better work?

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:55.980
Niceole: They can be for sure. There are people&nbsp;
in this business and I will just say that who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:03.420
are what we refer to as credit snobs right so&nbsp;
for example when I was coming up and I had done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:10.020
four broadcast shows in a row there were people at&nbsp;
some of the streamers and stuff who were like well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:15.420
but she's only done broadcast well then I went&nbsp;
and did Cloak and Dagger and I wrote a movie and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:21.360
suddenly I was really cool right because I had a&nbsp;
cool credit and I had written a movie and I'm like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:27.180
what I did in those procedural rooms is&nbsp;
harder than any of the stuff that you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:36.360
like oh she's so cool for because when you have&nbsp;
to break a crime 16,18, 22 times with the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:41.220
character solving it but you have to find a way&nbsp;
to make it cool and different every week trust me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:45.240
that's harder than anything you're going to&nbsp;
ask me to write in any of your snobby rooms

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:56.040
certainly say that once I transition to more&nbsp;
streaming content and that kind of stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:00.540
the stuff that was offered to me&nbsp;
definitely changed a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:08.100
so the fancy credits can definitely help&nbsp;
you get to that place you want to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:15.240
what I always tell newer writers is it used to&nbsp;
be that you couldn't get jobs on the fancy shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:20.220
but now you can because there's so many shows&nbsp;
right and they sometimes still hire staff writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:26.580
but think about your career long term so it's&nbsp;
great that you did a couple of streaming shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:34.560
and you got fancy credits but you probably didn't&nbsp;
go to set you probably don't barely saw any cuts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:41.820
of the show or you definitely didn't get to go to&nbsp;
post so your career needs those skills so that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:46.080
why I tell them like if you did a couple&nbsp;
of fancy shows go do a season a broadcast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:50.520
and first of all you're going to make better&nbsp;
money because you just make better money in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:54.720
broadcast and second you're going to get a&nbsp;
chance to do all that stuff you didn't get to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.740 --> 00:11:02.400
and people broadcast is its own animal&nbsp;
and it's a different energy and there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:08.760
something really pleasing about breaking a&nbsp;
story writing a story going to see it shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:15.540
and it making it on the air in a reasonable&nbsp;
amount of time it's just really joyful for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:21.960
your friends to not constantly be like when is&nbsp;
your show coming out you're like I don't know so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:30.180
the fancy credits will help you get some of&nbsp;
the cooler jobs but they will not necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:36.240
build the skill set that you need to become a show&nbsp;
runner and run your own show so I just tell people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:39.540
to always think about that when they're looking&nbsp;
at the jobs that are getting offered to them.

